// C++-interop coverage: the CFlat BRIDGE. Everything here is CFlat code that C++ sees -
// CFlat-defined [cpp] structs (plain, derived, generic), CFlat functions reached through
// the reverse C++ ABI, and std::function handed a CFlat callable. New bridge work goes
// in this file. The plain C++ surface is in Test/test_cpp_interop.cb; templates and std::
// specializations are in Test/test_cpp_interop_template.cb.

// End-to-end C++-interop coverage. Each section exercises one path of the C++ interop
// pipeline; on failure the section's exit code identifies which one regressed.
//   CFlat-defined no-base [cpp] structs - fields, layout, structors, ownership, move,
//     nesting, methods, static methods, and ctor edges.
//   CFlat-defined derived [cpp] structs - one C++ base, construction, overrides, virtual
//     dispatch, protected access, and diagnostics.
//   C++ struct return adoption, destructor counts, and closure rows.
//   C++ class-template specializations as [cpp] struct bases, including base initializers,
//     virtual dispatch, move cleanup, and fields.
//   C++ by-value return temporaries and inherited member-template lookup, including
//     constructor and sret returns, nested returns, unrelated temps, and implicit/explicit
//     receiver spellings.
//   std::make_shared/std::shared_ptr of CFlat-defined [cpp] structs, generated
//     constructors/destructors, virtual dispatch, conversions, recursive template arguments,
//     incomplete ordering, move ownership, promotion, receiver sret, member-template
//     overloads, and ref/value rank.
//   Generic CFlat-defined [cpp] structs - one C++ class per instantiation, dependent C++ base
//     initializers, override dispatch, destructor counts, move construction, and a no-base
//     field generic.
//   Initializing a local of a NONTRIVIAL C++ class from an lvalue that is not a bare
//     identifier - a dot field, an arrow field, a dereference, a namespace-scope object and a
//     vector element - must copy-CONSTRUCT. Every leg reads a refcount or a heap string the
//     source still owns, so a bitwise store (the pre-fix lowering) shows up as a low
//     use_count and a double release.
//   std::function as a C++ MEMBER parameter (by value, by const&, by &&, on a static member,
//     on a constructor) and as a RETURN type (free function, member, static member; called
//     directly and through a CFlat local), plus a member that STORES the callable and runs it
//     after the CFlat frame that made it is gone, the void() and int(const std::string&)
//     signatures, and the free-function forms of the same signatures already covered by the
//     plain-CFlat-function checks. Every member position was refused ("takes/returns
//     unsupported type") while the free-function parameter one bound.
//   CFlat by-value holder parameters with nontrivial C++ fields: read, mutation isolation,
//     nested and multiple fields, last-use move, loop copies, explicit move, return, recursion,
//     and calls through function values.
//   Owning lvalue ternaries at assignment sinks select storage for direct, nested, field,
//     array-element, and dereference arms; C++ fields copy-assign once, and explicit move
//     consumes only the selected C++ class arm.
//   Global CFlat structs with C++ fields construct each field before main through nested
//     structs and fixed arrays, preserve imported-module order, and keep global lifetime
//     without exit-time destruction. Regular, local-static, and [cpp] neighbours are covered.
//   Interface dispatch with CFlat holder and bare C++ class parameters by value: copy, last-use
//     move, loop copies, explicit move, holder return, composed C++ sret return, two implementors,
//     and a generic interface.
//     Plain CFlat aggregate and bare-class interface calls pin the existing ABI neighbours.
//   C++ sink parameter checks (2060-2079) for a CFlat-defined [cpp] derived struct: push_back
//     refuses an implicit copy, while explicit move, temporaries, emplace_back, and const-ref borrowing work.
//   C++ container members over deleted-copy keys and elements (2090-2099): set/map lookups by
//     const-ref lvalue, insert by move and temporary, map emplace, and emplace_back(move) bind.
//   C++ override helper ABI returns (2100-2109): void, scalar, pointer, reference, 8/16-byte
//     aggregates, and sret, with the adjacent scalar forms kept as value-checking neighbours.
//   C++ contiguous container -> CFlat T[] call-site decay (3510-3519): vectors, references,
//     temporaries, array, string, span, nested calls, fields, mutation, exact elements and rank.
//   C++ proxy prvalue assignment and binary operators (2100-2129): assignment and conversion cases
//     mutate the temporary's referent, temporary destruction is exact, and C++ member/free/builtin
//     comparisons bind while the implicit-only assignment remains an error.
//   std::set::insert of a generated, copyable [cpp] struct accepts a temporary and preserves
//     its set size and element value (3540-3549).
//   `unique T*` / `unique<T>` on a C++ class is std::unique_ptr<T> (ruling R5): adoption of `new`,
//     raw owners and nullptr, C++ move, fields, returns, sinks, and exact structor counts (3550-3559).
//   SFINAE member probes on CFlat-defined [cpp] methods and non-template forwarding calls
//     (3560-3569), including keyword-name omission, inherited overload visibility, and
//     same-signature CFlat hiding through C++ templates.
//   CFlat methods on a [cpp] derived struct hide same-named protected C++ base members for
//     direct, pointer, and in-method calls (3570-3579).
//   Nontrivial C++ field defaults in a generated [cpp] constructor construct in place for
//     direct initialization, declaration defaults, and `= default` (3580-3589).
//   CFlat by-value C++ unique_ptr parameters accept prvalue arguments and their returned
//     temporaries move into locals once, for `new`, C++ call results, and both type spellings
//     (3590-3599).
//   list<unique C++*> uses std::unique_ptr values for sinks, borrowing, take, release, growth,
//     and destruction, with exact C++ destructor counts (3600-3609).
//   C++ overload resolution compares each argument's conversion sequence on its own: equal
//     user conversions defer to the next argument, and an unsuffixed literal is a double, for
//     free functions and for members of an imported class, same-rank tie-breakers (3620-3630).
//   Moving core and C++ unique owners of a derived class into a C++ base-pointer sink adjusts
//     after release and destroys once; the exact-base owner remains unchanged (3610-3619).

